PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The requirements are (1) equal control via sole directorships held by Alex and Tyler plus 50/50 voting shares, (2) Alex compensated solely by salary (his shares must therefore carry no dividend rights), (3) Tyler compensated by dividends (his shares must be dividend-eligible), and (4) authorization of a usable non-voting class for future passive investors.

Only C meets every element at once: Alex and Tyler are the sole directors; Alex receives non-dividend Class A shares while Tyler receives dividend-eligible Class B shares; Class C is explicitly non-voting. All other options fail at least one criterion (E adds extra directors destroying equal control; A routes Alex through a holding company and gives both classes identical dividend rights; B reverses the dividend assignment; D authorizes no non-voting class at all). The limited economic rights on C's Class C shares are a minor drafting inconsistency that does not outweigh C's otherwise perfect alignment with the full set of stated needs.}

