7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues pushing C keep repeating that the articles must hard-wire a permanent dividend split and that E’s initial board immediately violates equal control. Both claims over-read the problem. The facts only say Alex prefers salary and Tyler prefers dividends; nothing requires the share classes themselves to prohibit one or the other. Salary can be paid under an employment contract or director fees regardless of dividend eligibility, and dividends can be declared selectively on identical classes. E therefore supplies the needed flexibility without forcing an artificial structural lock-in that none of the stated goals actually demand.

On the board point, the 50/50 voting shares in E give Alex and Tyler the power to reconstitute the board by ordinary resolution at any time. The presence of Tyler’s sons at incorporation is therefore a temporary drafting choice, not a permanent loss of equal control. By contrast, C’s Class C shares are drafted with no dividend or liquidation rights at all—an obvious flaw that would make them unattractive to the very outside investors the partners want to attract. C also introduces an unnecessary fourth class (Class D) whose voting rights are described inconsistently with the rest of the option. Those defects cannot be fixed without later amendments, undermining the “no later fixes” argument that C’s supporters rely on.

E already authorizes a usable non-voting class that still carries economic rights investors will accept, preserves 50/50 voting control, and leaves compensation routes open to ordinary corporate and tax planning. That combination satisfies every explicit requirement with the fewest structural complications. The other options have already been shown to reverse or duplicate rights or omit a workable investor class. E remains the cleanest fit.}

