PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues supporting C continue to insist that the articles must permanently strip Alex’s shares of dividend rights and that E’s initial board composition immediately destroys equal control. Both points over-read the problem statement. The facts only require that Alex prefers salary and Tyler prefers dividends; nothing forces an artificial structural prohibition into the share classes themselves. Salary can be paid under a standard employment agreement or director fees regardless of dividend eligibility on the shares, and dividends can be declared selectively even on identical voting classes. That flexibility is precisely what E preserves without locking the partners into an inflexible capital structure they may later need to amend.

On the board issue, the 50/50 Class A and B voting shares in E give Alex and Tyler the power to reconstitute the board by ordinary resolution at any time after incorporation. The presence of Tyler’s sons is therefore a temporary drafting choice that the founders can correct without amending the articles. By contrast, C’s Class C shares are drafted with no dividend or liquidation rights whatsoever—an obvious flaw that would render them unattractive to the very outside investors the partners want to attract. C also introduces an unnecessary fourth class (Class D) whose voting rights appear inconsistently described relative to the rest of the option. Those defects would require later amendments, undermining the “no later fixes” argument C’s supporters rely on.

E already authorizes a usable non-voting class that still carries meaningful economic rights investors will accept, preserves strict 50/50 voting control between Alex and Tyler, and leaves compensation routes open to ordinary corporate and tax planning. That combination satisfies every explicit requirement with the fewest structural complications. The other options have already been shown to reverse or duplicate rights or omit a workable investor class. E remains the cleanest fit.}

